{"kind":"request","text":"Resolve the object-constructor key-probe dispatch rule for the I7 extension path before it is admitted. This is a bounded semantic clarification under the full existing #4043 outcome; implementation and eventual pin/callers stay on Builder's live promise.\n\nThe adopted note requires no speculative/repeated evaluation of a logical provider call, while preserving the pinned language and existing refusals. Actual pinned599f Evaluate executes an object-key expression once against the whole input before evaluating its per-item keys (v206/jsonata.go:2645); our private meter_group_keys.go preserves that behavior. With a pure expression-local probe returning its string argument, input[1,2] and {$count($)\u003e1 ? $probe(\"probe-only\") : \"k\":$} return {\"k\":[1,2]} but invoke probe once, even though neither item takes that branch. {$probe(\"key\"):$} invokes three times for two items. Controls give two calls for two explicit identical sites, zero for a false branch, two in the bounded two-item path, and one in a grouped value. The same six exact JSON outputs/traces were reproduced from the current fork c9aa9de4 and the untouched pinned upstream dependency, with byte-identical results. These are isolated legacy expression-local callback probes, not admitted providers or evidence of completed metered extension integration.\n\nPlease record the governing resolution. My proposed implementation is to suppress the preliminary whole-input key probe only for a key AST containing a selected extension call, then perform normal per-item key evaluation with exact call/work charging and ordinary per-item key-type checks. Retain the legacy preliminary probe for all keys without selected extensions, and use no memoization. This satisfies the no-speculation rule, but explicitly chooses how new extension-bearing keys behave: a mixed key whose whole-input pure branch is non-string could now reach valid per-item extension-produced string keys instead of the legacy